   "effort": "medium",
   "sample": 1,
   "answered": true,
   "answer": "My lord, a sonnet is a poem of fourteen lines written in iambic pentameter. It is traditionally divided into two parts: the first eight lines, called an octave, present a problem or idea; the final six lines, known as the sestet, offer a resolution or counterpoint. The rhyme scheme varies by tradition—English (Shakespearean) sonnets follow ABAB CDCD EFEF GG, whereas Italian (Petrarchan) sonnets use ABBAABBA for the octave and then either CDECDE or CDCDCD for the sestet. The form is prized for its compactness and the tension it creates between a statement and its rebuttal.",
