   "case": "F-sonnet",
   "class": "F",
   "effort": "low",
   "sample": 2,
   "answered": true,
   "answer": "A sonnet is a poetic form with a strict metrical and structural pattern, traditionally written in iambic pentameter. It consists of 14 lines divided into two main sections: a quatrain (four-line stanza) and a sestet (six‑line stanza). The rhyme scheme in the most common English form, the Shakespearean sonnet, follows ABAB CDCD EFEF GG. In the Petrarchan (Italian) form, it is typically ABBA CDCD EFFE or ABBA CDCD CDEE.\n\nThe meter, iambic pentameter, means each line contains ten syllables in alternating unstressed–stressed feet. This rhythmic regularity lends the sonnet its musical quality.\n\nHistorically, the form originated in Italy in the early 13th century with poets such as Giacomo da Lentini, who is credited with creating the 14‑line structure. It was later adapted by Petrarch in his \"Canzoni,\" which popularized the Italian rhyme scheme. By the late 16th century, English poets such as William Shakespeare had transformed it into a distinctively Elizabethan style. Each tradition emphasizes different thematic focuses—love and beauty in the Italian, broader social commentary in Shakespeare’s works.\n\nA sonnet is often viewed as a self‑contained argument or meditation: the quatrain(s) present an idea, the sestet develops it, and the final couplet delivers a resolution or surprising turn. Because of its compactness, the sonnet demands precision; every word must serve both meaning and form.\n\nThere are also variations—such as the Spenserian sonnet (ABABBCBCCDCDEE) or the modern “sonnet‑in‑verse” where the physical shape contributes to meaning—but all retain the core features of 14 lines, iambic pentameter (or a close approximation), and a rhyme scheme that offers unity and contrast.",
